Genera documentation: what to read, and in what order

These are Symbolics manuals for Genera 8 and Open Genera 2.0, the
system this emulator runs (Open Genera 2.0 is Genera 8.5 on a Virtual
Lisp Machine). They come from
http://bitsavers.org/pdf/symbolics/software/genera_8/ and remain
copyright Symbolics.

The manuals were written for Symbolics hardware and for Open Genera on
a DEC Alpha under Digital Unix. Everything about Genera itself applies
unchanged. Host-side instructions (installation, packet filter, Unix
commands) do not; for those follow the README in the top directory.

Once Genera can reach the sys.sct tree, all of this and much more is
also on line in the Document Examiner (SELECT D).


1. Getting it running

   Read these before the first boot.

   Open_Genera_User_s_Guide.pdf (29 pages)
       What Open Genera is and how Genera and the host fit together.

   Open_Genera_Installation_Guide.pdf (56 pages)
       Read for the concepts: worlds, the distribution site, defining
       your own site, how Genera reaches the host's files. The Digital
       Unix commands themselves do not apply to Linux.

   Open_Genera_Beta_II_Release_Notes.pdf (15 pages)
       Short; skim for known limitations.


2. Learning to use Genera

   Read in this order. This is all a user who is not programming needs.

   Genera_Concepts.pdf (26 pages)
       The big picture. Read first; it is short and explains why
       Genera does not behave like Unix.

   Genera_Workbook.pdf (132 pages)
       The hands-on tutorial. Work through it at the running system.

   Genera_User_s_Guide.pdf (190 pages)
       The full user's manual: the keyboard, the mouse, windows, the
       command processor, files, getting help.

   Genera_Handbook.pdf (188 pages)
       Look-up companion to the User's Guide. Keep at hand; not for
       reading straight through.

   Genera_8.0_Reference_Cards.pdf (35 pages)
       Key bindings and commands at a glance. Print it.

   Editing_and_Mail.pdf (358 pages)
       Zmacs, the editor, and Zmail. Read the Zmacs introduction once
       you start editing files; the rest is reference.


3. Programming

   Program_Development_Tutorial.pdf (63 pages)
       Read first. Builds the game of Life step by step and shows the
       edit, compile, run and debug cycle.

   Symbolics_Common_Lisp_Language_Concepts.pdf (443 pages)
       The language. Start with its "Overview of Symbolics Common
       Lisp", which is meant to be read in one sitting.

   Symbolics_Common_Lisp_Programming_Constructs.pdf (387 pages)
   Symbolics_Common_Lisp_Dictionary.pdf (803 pages)
       Reference: the first by topic, the second alphabetical.

   Program_Development_Utilities.pdf (420 pages)
       Reference for the System Construction Tool, the compiler, the
       debugger and related tools.

   Getting_Ready_for_CLOS.pdf (25 pages)
       Flavors compared with CLOS. Read if you know one of them.

   Programming_the_User_Interface.pdf (517 pages)
   User_Interface_Dictionary.pdf (481 pages)
       Windows, presentations and commands for your own programs:
       concepts in the first, alphabetical reference in the second.

   CLIM_2.0_Release_Notes_and_Installation_Guide.pdf (9 pages)
       Only if you intend to use CLIM.


4. Administration and networking

   Read when you move beyond the distribution site: defining a site,
   saving your own world, talking to other hosts.

   Site_Operations.pdf (440 pages)
       Sites, namespaces, saving worlds, loading patches. The chapters
       on setting up a site are the ones to read; much of the rest
       concerns hardware this emulator does not have.

   Symbolics_Network_File_System__NFS__User_s_Guide.pdf (23 pages)
       How Genera reads the host's files. Relevant here, because NFS
       is the only way Genera reaches sys.sct.

   Symbolics_IPTCP_Software_Package.pdf (35 pages)
   Networks.pdf (261 pages)
       IP networking in Genera, then networking in general. Reference.

   Symbolics_X_Window_System_User_s_Guide.pdf (21 pages)
       Genera's X client and server. Optional.


5. How it works inside

   Internals.pdf (267 pages)
       The scheduler, storage management and other internals. Not
       needed to use the system.

   ../documentation/64-bit-porting-guide.txt
   ../documentation/vlm-installation.txt
       Notes on the emulator itself, from its original authors. The
       installation notes describe the 1990s Digital Unix setup and
       are of historical interest only.


The shortest useful path: Genera Concepts, then the Genera Workbook,
then the Program Development Tutorial.
